+## pm61 (current unit) — full detail
+
+**pm61 (Upload: service + action): IMPLEMENTED, unit-tested, NOT yet run against a live DB this session (started and code-complete 2026-09-28).** Part 5. Boundary per `specs/pm61-upload-service-and-action.md`: `services/product/ratecard/upload-version.ts` (new), `actions/product/upload-ratecard-version.action.ts` (new — `PRODUCT_ACTION_FILES` +1, guardrail updated same commit), the `RATECARD_VERSION_UPLOADED` audit type + its full three-part ripple, and this unit's tests (one DB-free action suite, green; one live-DB suite, written but not executed — see the DB-access note below). No UI (pm66), no diff (pm62), no activation (pm63).
+
+**G-RC3 resolved in this change set (its real deadline, per `pm00-build-plan.md`'s own gate table).** Took the recommended option: a new `ratecard` permission (READ/EDIT only, no DELETE), added to `types/rbac.ts` `PERMISSION_NAMES` and `auth/permission-constants.ts` `PERMISSIONS.RATECARD`, seeded via a new migration `0043_ratecard_permission.sql` (the pm23/bm01 "custom SQL, `INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`" pattern — no `drizzle-kit generate`, no snapshot file, matching pm57a's finding that this migrator reads no snapshot at runtime). `types/permissions.ts`'s `EffectivePermissionMap` gained `ratecard` to its `OptionalPermissionName` union (the exact mechanism that keeps a new module permission from forcing an update to every hardcoded test fixture — this is what kept the ripple small) and `types/roles.ts` `PERMISSION_DISPLAY_NAMES` gained `ratecard: "Rate Card"` (required — the permission-matrix UI enumerates every name unconditionally). Ripple actually needed, found via `tsc` plus a manual sweep for the array-length assertions `tsc` never catches (per `[[permission-name-addition-ripple]]`): `tests/components/permission-matrix-editor.test.tsx` and `tests/components/role-detail.test.tsx` (14→15 rows, "Rate Card" appended), `tests/services/roles-read.service.test.ts` and its `.integration.test.ts` twin (three `mappings` array literals + one length assertion), `tests/auth/resolver.test.ts` (four full-map `toEqual` literals). Full suite re-run green after (315 tests / 27 files touched by the ripple, all passing). **Role grants are not yet seeded** (no `db:seed-ratecard` script exists) — deferred; pm65/pm66 (nav + authz matrix, per G-RC3's own "also the nav entry and authz matrix" note) or a follow-up is the natural place, matching pm25/bm01's own split of "permission row in the migration, grants in a seed."
+
+**`cardName` is a real per-upload input, not a hardcoded constant.** `pm00-build-plan.md`'s pm66 row describes `UploadVersionDialog` with a "card-name select" — confirming multiple `card_name` values are a first-class (if currently single-seeded, D-A5) input, not something this service should assume. The action reads it from `FormData` (`formData.get("cardName")`, trimmed, required) alongside the file.
+
+**Two spec/reality tensions found and resolved by adding, not by inventing a fourth violation (raised, not silently patched):**
+1. **The checksum-duplicate warning cannot literally be `RateCardIssue[]`.** `RateCardIssue.violation` is `RateCardUploadViolation`, closed to exactly three structural members (pm58 D4) — a checksum match is not one of them and workflow §5.4 forbids a fourth. Added a sibling type, `RateCardUploadWarning` (same `line`/`column`/`value`/`reason` shape, no `violation` field), exported from `upload-version.ts`; the success result's `warnings` field is typed with it instead. pm66 renders it in the same table shape.
+2. **A concurrent-upload race needs a result code that isn't a structural violation either.** I5.8's "two uploads racing when no draft exists... the loser surfaces a typed refusal, not a raw `23505`" requires catching a unique-violation on `ratecard_version_one_draft_per_card` (via the existing `lib/db-errors.ts` `isUniqueViolation` helper — the same pattern `insert-price.ts`/`update-price.ts` already use for their own constraint). Added `{ ok: false; code: "CONCURRENT_UPLOAD_CONFLICT" }` as a result-union member distinct from `RateCardUploadViolation` (the closed set stays exactly three; this is a concurrency outcome, not a content defect) — threaded through both the service's and the action's result types.
+
+**Design notes worth recording:** the D3 adapter from pm59's header-keyed `cells` to pm58's positional `{header, rows: string[][]}` is built from `parsed.header` (order-preserving, duplicates intact) rather than `RATE_CARD_HEADER_MAP`'s canonical order, so a duplicate/unknown header is caught from the raw header array before any row's `cells` lookup could be affected by a duplicate-key collision. D12's `deleteDraftVersion` → re-read `max(version_num)` on `tx` ordering is implemented literally (the second `listVersions` call only fires when a draft was actually deleted, so the no-existing-draft path costs nothing extra). The one-transaction budget is: 1–2 `listVersions` + optional `deleteDraftVersion` + `insertVersion` + N/1000 `insertLookupRows` batches + `insertAuditEvent` — bounded by batch count, not row count.
+
+**DB access this session — flagged, not run.** Docker's `db` container was up (2 days) but the local dev stack itself was not in the state `[[local-docker-dev-stack]]` documents: migrations were only applied through `0039` (nothing from `0040` onward, including pm57a's own `0041`), `app_runtime`'s password didn't match `.env` (fixed in-place via `ALTER ROLE`, a reversible dev-only change), and `app_runtime` is not the owner of at least the `billing` schema (a `DROP SCHEMA` as `app_runtime` — what every `*.integration.test.ts` file does in `beforeAll` — fails with "must be owner"). Per `[[local-docker-dev-stack]]`'s own explicit warning, running any integration suite against this shared stack drops schemas and (in the full `npm run test`) rewrites cluster role passwords and drops the `kestra` database — destructive to a stack this session did not stand up and was not asked to reset. Rather than do that unilaterally, the live-DB suite (`tests/db/ratecard-upload-version.integration.test.ts`) was written to the same standard as pm60's (query-budget counter, checksum-warning isolation, D12 replace-draft proof, the concurrent-race proof, the D4 timezone proof) but **not executed this session**. `tsc --noEmit`, ESLint and Prettier are clean repo-wide; the DB-free action suite (`tests/actions/upload-ratecard-version.action.test.ts`, 13 cases) is green.
+
+**FINDING — `db/migrations/0042_cold_paibok.sql` will break a migrate-from-empty, independent of this unit.** It was added in `7e6bc26` ("chore(deps): remediate npm audit"), and `db/migrations/meta/0042_snapshot.json` exists alongside it — meaning `drizzle-kit generate` was run against the stale `meta/0026`-era snapshot floor (pm57a's own finding: "snapshots stop at 0026... `drizzle-kit generate` is retired project-wide"). The generated file re-issues `CREATE SEQUENCE product.ratecard_version_seq`, `CREATE TABLE product.ratecard_version`, `CREATE TABLE product.ratecard_ran_usage_lkp` and both ratecard partial unique indexes — all already owned by `0041` (pm57a's rewritten shape) — alongside a large, apparently legitimate batch of unrelated schema (the pm46 price-component reshape, the `TESTING`/`OBSOLETE` enum values, several billing/rating tables). Journal order (`0041.when < 0042.when`) means a fresh `npm run db:migrate` applies `0041` successfully and then fails on `0042`'s duplicate `CREATE TABLE`. This is not a ratecard-only file and touches schema owned by other modules, so it is **out of pm61's boundary** (pm61 explicitly makes no schema change) — raised here rather than silently patched or silently worked around. Whoever next needs a clean migrate-from-empty (this unit's own live-DB suite included) will need this resolved first — the likely fix is removing only the six ratecard-related statements from `0042`, keeping the rest.
+
